  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 823 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThe aforementioned front will continue to slowly push ESE across\nthe Tennessee Valley Saturday night into Sunday, with medium \nchances (40-60%) of showers and a few storms moving ahead of and\nalong the boundary. WPC has placed much of the area under a\nMarginal Risk (Level 1/4) for excessive rainfall impacts, meaning\nthat a low chance of flooding will persist into Sunday, with\nlocally heavy rainfall occurring with some of this activity \n(thanks to PWATs near 2\"). The front should begin to sag south \nlate in the day and into the evening and overnight hours, as a\nstronger longwave trough over the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley \nfinally nudges it to the southeast. In it's wake, expect a cooler,\ndrier air mass to move in early next week and a surface high \nbuilding in from the north. This will mean beautiful autumn \nweather for the first week in October, with highs in the lower 80s\nand lows in the low to mid 50s.",
